label gate
[signal arg="bg:academy_gate"]
The academy sat on the open plain outside the capital like something that had fallen out of the sky.
Domed faculty halls in the morning fog, the Colosseum crouched behind them, four dorm buildings in neat rows.
Lan (Sus): (A hundred and seventeen years since the war ended. First year, first day. The clock starts now.)
Enriko: Okay, okay, okay, hold on. That is a sword.
Lan (Talk): It's a tent pole.
Enriko: It's a sword, and it's enormous, and you're carrying it like you're off to go fishing. Enriko.
Enriko: Support Department, Nusantara, first year, extremely rich, terrible at fighting. You're Physical, obviously.
Enriko: Look at your shoulders. What's your rank?
Lan (Default): {rank}.
Enriko: Perfect. I'm seventy-one. We're both garbage. We're going to get along great.
Enriko: Lesson one, free of charge. Don't stand in front of the notice board by the east arch.
Enriko: That's where people go to have feelings about the rank list. Read it at night, like a normal coward.
Enriko: Lesson two. The girl in the third-year courtyard with the black gloves? Don't talk to her.
Enriko: She once ignored a lord's son so completely that he transferred schools.
Lan (Talk): How many lessons are there?
Enriko: Forty. I've been here three hours.
Lan (Smile): (I laughed. A real one, the first in a while.)
Lan (Sus): (Careful. This is exactly how a story pulls you in.)
Lan (Sus): (Someone likable at the gate on day one, and you forget you're on a deadline. He isn't in my memory of the game.)
Lan (Sus): (Background. Harmless.)
set {aff_enriko} += 1
Enriko: Veldero! Over here! Come meet my new sword friend!
Veldero: Sorry. Are you two[pause=0.35][lspeed=0.25!]...[lspeed]?
Enriko: Best friends. It's been four minutes. Lan, Veldero. Veldero, Lan. He's Physical, he's got a tent pole, he's {rank}.
Veldero: Seventy-four. I almost didn't get in at all.
Lan (Sus): (I read the gi off his hand. Flat, low, ordinary. Commoner. Nothing in my memory about him.)
set {aff_veldero} += 1
Enriko: You're not going to ask about the sword? Everyone asks about the sword.
Veldero: I don't know enough about swords to have an opinion. It could be the best sword in the world or it could just be a tent pole.
Lan (Smile): See, he gets it.
Enriko: I refuse to accept this.
Veldero: It's clearly a very well-made tent pole.
label ceremony
[signal arg="bg:great_hall"]
Lan (Sus): (Eighty-four first-years. He's one of them.)
Lan (Sus): (He's always in the front section in this scene, I've seen it two thousand four hundred and fifty times. I know his face.)
The Britannic Arcana banner hung over the left aisle.
Under it sat a girl with red hair and gold-orange eyes, holding herself so still the students around her looked fidgety.
Crown Princess Leonora von Rosendael.
Near the front, in Al-Tale'an gray, a young man with white hair and a coiled kind of stillness. Raiz Al Khalid.
Against the far wall, a fourth-year with gold hair and blue eyes wearing a sword from a hundred loading screens. Richard Leonhard.
Lan (Sus): (All exactly right. So where's[pause=0.35][lspeed=0.25!]...[lspeed])
He went face by face through the front section, row by row. Not an empty seat. Every seat full. No gap a stranger would ever notice.
Lan (Mad): (The room looks correct. The room is lying.)
Lan (Sus): (Late. Sick. Deferred a term. There's a version where he walks in Thursday and everything is fine.)
Lan (Sus): (I don't believe it.)
Berthram: I'm required to give you the speech.
Berthram: It says you're the finest young talents on the continent,
Berthram: that this academy has stood a hundred and seventeen years, and that great things are expected of you.
Berthram: All true. Also useless. So here's the real one.
Berthram: There are eighty-four of you.
Berthram: There's a number next to every one of your names, and about a third of you will decide it means something about who you are as a person.
Berthram: It doesn't. It means you had one good morning, or one bad one, in front of three tired examiners.
Berthram: I've buried a lot of students who came in ranked high.
Berthram: I've buried fewer who came in ranked low, and that's because they were careful, and they knew they had to be.
Berthram: Out there, the thing trying to kill you doesn't check your rank first. It cares how much of your energy you waste.
Berthram: It cares whether your feet are in the right place. It cares whether the person next to you is still standing.
Berthram: Come see me if you're in trouble. Come see me before you're in trouble, which none of you will do. Classes start Monday. Welcome to Tausend Sterne.
Richard: I'm told I'm supposed to give a speech too. Unlike the Headmaster's, I actually wrote mine down.
Richard: I sat where you're sitting four years ago. I was rank eleven, which felt like the end of the world, and I have never looked at that number again.
Richard: Nobody in this hall got here by accident.
Richard: Whatever door you walked through, money, talent, blood, or nothing but stubbornness, something in you refused to quit.
Richard: Hold on to that. Measure yourself against who you were yesterday. That's the only fight you can win.
Richard: Good luck. Try not to set the dormitories on fire in the first week. That one's a personal request.
Lan (Smug): (Chosen one. Yeah. That checks out.)
label board
[signal arg="bg:rank_board_night"]
The list went up at four.
Lan waited until nine, the way Enriko had told him to,
and walked to the east arch once the lanterns were lit and the courtyard had gone quiet except for a cat on the wall.
Eighty-four names, black ink on white board behind glass. He found his own first. {rank}. SCHWERT, L. Physical.
Then he went looking for the one that mattered. One, Raiz Al Khalid. Two, a minor rival from later in the story.
Three, Leonora von Rosendael. Four, a name that meant nothing to him. Five.
Lan (Mad): (It isn't him.)
Someone else stood in the one spot that had belonged to exactly one person for two thousand four hundred and fifty hours.
He kept going. Six was the minor rival, one place lower than he remembered. Seven, one slot down.
Every name from six onward had slid down the board by exactly one place, patient and orderly, like water finding a new level.
Lan (Sus): (Not a bump. A shift.)
Lan (Sus): (Exactly one person is missing, and the whole list rearranged itself around the hole so cleanly nobody would notice unless they'd memorized the roster.)
At the bottom, eighty-four was a name he did not recognize.
Not a rival, not a background face, not a line of flavor text in two thousand four hundred and fifty hours.
Someone pulled in from outside to keep the total tidy.
Lan (Sus): (One name erased. Everyone shifts up to hide it. One new name pulled in to keep the count even.)
Lan (Mad): (He's dead. Not late. Dead, before the semester even started, and whoever did it made the story close back over the hole like water.)
- Lay a hand on the glass
	Lan (Sus): (Come on. Be here.)
- Read the names again
	Lan (Sus): (Once more. Slower. There has to be a mistake.)
He put his hand flat over all eighty-four names, as if pressure alone could force the true one back into existence.
Lan (Sus): (Then I'm not the support character anymore.)
Lan (Sus): (Everything I built over seventeen years rested on one assumption, that someone else would carry the story and I'd only keep him alive.)
Lan (Sus): (An anchor only means something if there's a ship attached.)
Lan (Sus): (If he's really not here, the story isn't a fixed track anymore. Everything I know stops being knowledge and starts being a guess.)
He walked back to a small, clean dorm room with two beds. The other was claimed by someone who snored.
He laid the gray-wrapped bundle across his knees and did not unwrap it.
Lan (Sus): (One. The main character of this world isn't in this building, and I have no idea why. Two.)
Lan (Sus): (In under a month I sit in the Headmaster's office and answer honest questions about a family I know nothing about.)
Lan (Sus): (Three. I'm ranked {rank} of 84, still leaking more than half my gi, and the Evil God's awakening starts this year.)
Lan (Smile): Okay.
Lan (Smile): (And some small part of me, asleep under four years in a dark apartment and six in a cold forest,)
Lan (Smile): (stretched and woke up a little, and found that it was, very faintly, interested.)
Lan (Smug): (So it's my story after all.)
[signal arg="chapter_end:ch02"]
[end_timeline]
